            "is_markdown": true,
            "text": "## Summary\n\nOn a vector of length 2^31 - 1 or more, `x[c(\"a\", \"b\")] <- v` with new names and a single value writes `v` 2^31 elements before the vector, and only then fails. Fixes a reproducible out-of-bounds write: memory corruption from plain R code, though only with such large vectors.\n\n## Details\n\n`stringSubscript()` (`src/main/subscript.c`) gives each new name the next position past the end, counting in `R_xlen_t` but storing the positions in an `int` index. Past `INT_MAX` they wrap: 2^31 becomes `NA_INTEGER` and 2^31 + 1 becomes -2^31 + 1, so `VectorAssign()` writes at offset -2^31. On a long vector, `x <- raw(2^31); x[\"a\"] <- as.raw(1)` writes out of bounds too. Tested at r90991.\n\nReproducible example: attached as `string-subscript-overflow-repro.R` (about 20 GB without the patch). On macOS, a separate vector `y` gets a nonzero byte and the assignment fails in `SET_STRING_ELT()`; expected: `y` stays zero.\n\nProposed patch: attached as `string-subscript-overflow.diff`. `stringSubscript()` stores `NA` for positions past `INT_MAX`, so `x[names]` reads keep giving `NA`, and `VectorAssign()` raises \"long vectors not supported yet\" for such an index before it changes `x`. Adds a 2 GB test to `tests/reg-large.R`; `make check` passes on macOS arm64.\n\nThe patch is also submitted as https://github.com/r-devel/r-svn/pull/546, where CI results can be seen.\n\n## AI Disclosure\n\nThis patch was prepared with the assistance of Claude Code. The issue was discovered while reviewing a separate patch to `VectorAssign()`, https://github.com/r-devel/r-svn/pull/541.",
